<p>Before you read anything else, wait until your dog is lying still, ideally asleep, and count. Watch the side of the chest. One rise and one fall is a single breath. Count for thirty seconds, double it, and write the number down with the date and time.</p>

<p>That number matters, because "why is my dog breathing fast?" usually comes down to two things: what the dog was doing just beforehand, and how many breaths a minute he takes once he's supposed to be calm. A Labrador blowing hard after twenty minutes of fetch is doing exactly what his body was built to do. The same Labrador breathing fifty times a minute while asleep on the couch at midnight is telling you something different, even if he otherwise looks fine.</p>

<h2>What the Number Should Look Like</h2>

<p>Sources don't settle on one exact figure, so it helps to know the range they share. A veterinarian quoted by the American Kennel Club puts a normal resting respiratory rate at 20 to 35 breaths per minute and calls 40 to 60 breaths per minute "very concerning." PetMD describes normal, healthy breathing as deep, long breaths about 15 to 30 times a minute. It also goes further than the AKC: a rate usually above 30 while a dog is sleeping or resting is, in PetMD's words, a medical emergency, because it may point to a problem with the lungs, heart, or airways.</p>

<p>In practice, then, a sleeping dog breathing somewhere in the teens or twenties is in comfortable territory. A sleeping dog who is consistently above 30 needs a call to your vet. A dog breathing 40 or more times a minute at rest, especially if you can see effort in each breath, should not be watched and waited on.</p>

<p>Your own dog's baseline matters too. VCA Animal Hospitals, writing about congestive heart failure, suggests counting several times over a week to learn your dog's average resting rate. It adds that a significant increase should be reported to your vet even if the new number still falls inside the normal range for an average dog. The Merck Veterinary Manual notes that vets often ask owners to track this sleeping respiratory rate at home because it can pick up early heart failure. If you have an older dog, or a dog with a known heart murmur, that's a habit worth starting tonight.</p>

<h2>First Question: What Was Happening Ten Minutes Ago?</h2>

<p>Most panting has an obvious cause, and the cause usually comes just before it. Dogs don't cool themselves the way we do. VCA points out that they have relatively few sweat glands, mostly in their footpads, so panting is their main way to regulate body temperature. According to the AKC, moisture evaporating off the tongue and the lining of the airways carries heat away with it. Hence the panting on a warm afternoon or after a run.</p>

<p>Excitement does it too: the doorbell, the leash coming off its hook, you getting home. Then there's stress. The AKC's veterinary source describes patients who start panting from nothing more than being in the exam room, and dogs who pant heavily through thunder or fireworks. That panting is real distress rather than illness, and it usually comes with pacing, lip-licking, or hiding. If your dog's panting reliably lines up with storms, car rides, or being left alone, our <a href="/tools/dog-anxiety-guide/">dog anxiety guide</a> covers what helps.</p>

<p>Normal panting fades. After exercise or excitement, the AKC's vet says breathing should improve within five to ten minutes of rest, water, and shade. If the rate stays high past that, she recommends getting the dog to a clinic within 30 minutes. "It stopped once he cooled down" is reassuring. "It's been forty minutes and he's still going" is not.</p>

<h2>Second Question: Is the Mouth Open? (Dog Breathing Fast but Not Panting)</h2>

<p>Many owners don't realize panting and fast breathing are not the same thing.</p>

<p>PetMD describes panting as short, shallow, open-mouth breathing after exercise or in the heat. The tongue is out, the breaths are quick and light, and the purpose is cooling. Heavy or labored breathing is different: fast breaths that take visible extra effort, often with the mouth closed or only partly open. You see the belly working along with the chest. A dog breathing fast with his mouth shut, while lying in a cool room, isn't trying to shed heat. Something else is driving it.</p>

<p>That something can be the lungs filling with fluid. The Merck Veterinary Manual explains that left-sided congestive heart failure causes fluid to build up in the lungs, and that dogs with it often breathe faster than healthy dogs. It can also be pain. PetMD gives the example of pancreatitis, where abdominal pain alone can push breathing harder. Other possibilities include pneumonia, bronchitis, and anemia from sudden blood loss, because fewer red blood cells means less oxygen gets delivered with each breath.</p>

<p>The physical signs PetMD flags as emergencies are worth memorizing:</p>
<ul>
<li>The abdomen pushing hard as the dog breathes out, instead of the chest and belly moving together in an easy rhythm</li>
<li>Nostrils flaring heavily while the dog is at rest</li>
<li>Standing with the front legs set wide apart and the neck stretched out, a posture that helps the chest expand</li>
<li>Gums that look pale, gray, blue, or purple instead of pink</li>
</ul>

<p>Any one of those, paired with fast breathing at rest, is a reason to go to an emergency vet rather than make a phone call and wait.</p>

<h2>Third Question: What Is the Rest of the Body Doing?</h2>

<p>Whatever else is happening at the same time usually narrows things down faster than the breathing itself.</p>

<h3>Panting and shaking together</h3>

<p>This pairing has three common explanations, and context usually tells them apart. The first is fear. A dog with a known fear of storms who pants and trembles while thunder rolls is showing you exactly what's wrong. The second is pain, which owners often miss. The AKC's vet notes that dogs with arthritis frequently come into the clinic panting, and that back pain or abdominal discomfort often shows up as a dog who is panting and anxious. The other pain signs on the AKC's list include trembling, restlessness, vocalizing, limping, rapid breathing, and licking or chewing at one spot. A dog who pants, shakes, and won't settle on a quiet evening with no storm in sight may well be hurting.</p>

<p>The third is rarer but more urgent: poisoning or overheating. The AKC notes that some toxins, rat bait among them, can drive body temperature up, and it lists muscle tremors among the signs that need immediate veterinary care in a panting dog. If there's any chance your dog got into medication, a cleaning product, or a pest-control product, our <a href="/tools/pet-poison-emergency-guide/">pet poison emergency guide</a> lists who to call and what information to have ready.</p>

<h3>Coughing alongside the breathing</h3>

<p>VCA lists coughing while resting or sleeping, an increased resting breathing rate or excessive panting, tiring easily on walks, a swollen belly, and pale or bluish gums as signs associated with heart failure. Merck adds that some dogs with left-sided heart failure faint. A dog who coughs at night and breathes fast while asleep needs a vet appointment soon, not someday.</p>

<h3>The gums</h3>

<p>Lift your dog's lip and look. The AKC's vet says healthy gums should be pale pink. Dark red, blue, or purple gums suggest the dog isn't getting enough oxygen and needs treatment right away.</p>

<h2>Dog Panting at Night, or While Resting, With No Obvious Reason</h2>

<p>"No reason" usually means "no reason I can see yet." Start with the boring checks: a warm room, a spot against the radiator, a long evening walk. If none of that explains it, the likely causes depend on the dog's age and history.</p>

<p>In middle-aged and senior dogs, the AKC points to Cushing's disease, an overproduction of the stress hormone cortisol, as a known cause of panting at rest. PetMD notes that it usually comes with increased thirst and urination and often a pot-bellied look from weakened abdominal muscles. The AKC's vet describes it as slow and not urgent, but still worth addressing, so a dog who pants, drinks, and pees more over a few months is due a scheduled vet visit.</p>

<p>Pain is the next possibility, especially in older dogs with stiff joints who can't get comfortable lying down. Heart disease is the one not to miss. PetMD says that heavy breathing at night, combined with pacing, coughing, an outstretched neck, gum color changes, or trouble getting comfortable, can signal congestive heart failure and should be checked as soon as possible.</p>

<p>Puppies are a gentler case. PetMD notes that puppies often breathe heavily during sleep because they're dreaming, and that a puppy who's bright, playful, and eating well probably has nothing wrong. If you're counting breaths, wait for deep, still sleep rather than a twitchy dream phase, when paws paddle and breathing speeds up for a minute or two before settling again.</p>

<h2>Flat-Faced Breeds Play by Different Rules</h2>

<p>If you share your home with a pug, French or English bulldog, Boston terrier, shih tzu, Pekingese, or boxer, the standard advice needs adjusting. VCA explains that brachycephalic dogs have shortened skull bones, and many have narrowed nostrils, an overly long soft palate, or a smaller-than-normal windpipe. Together these make up brachycephalic obstructive airway syndrome, or BOAS, and they make every breath take more effort.</p>

<p>For these dogs, some noise is baseline. VCA notes that mildly affected dogs breathe noisily, especially during exercise, and most snort when excited and snore when relaxed or asleep. Many also breathe more easily through the mouth than the nose. But "normal for a bulldog" is not the same as "harmless." More severely affected dogs tire quickly, and some collapse or faint after exercise. Their ability to pant and cool down is limited, so they overheat easily. VCA's heatstroke guidance warns that flat-faced breeds can show heatstroke signs when the temperature and humidity are only moderately high.</p>

<p>So learn what your dog sounds like on a good day. A new rasp, more effort than usual, or panting that doesn't settle after a short walk means something has changed. Heat precautions also need to start earlier in the season for these dogs.</p>

<h2>When the Panting Is Heatstroke</h2>

<p>Heatstroke is the emergency hidden inside "my dog is panting a lot," and it can develop in minutes, especially in a parked car. The AKC describes three stages. Heat stress shows up as mild panting and thirst. Heat exhaustion brings heavy panting, drooling, and fatigue. Heatstroke itself involves collapse or other severe signs. Early on, the gums and tongue often turn bright red. As it progresses, they may turn pale or bluish, and the dog may collapse.</p>

<p>Numbers help here. The AKC gives a normal canine body temperature of 99.5 to 102.5°F and says heatstroke typically sets in above 105.8°F. VCA considers anything over 103°F abnormal. Beyond hot cars, VCA lists yards without shade or water, long hair-dryer sessions, and hard exercise in the heat, and notes that a muzzle raises the risk by restricting panting.</p>

<p>If you suspect heatstroke, VCA's guidance is to pour cool water, not cold, over the head, stomach, armpits, and feet, and to mist the dog and put them in front of a fan or air conditioning. Don't drape wet towels over the dog, because they can trap heat. Ice packs and rubbing alcohol are no longer recommended, and the AKC's vet also warns against ice baths. Head to a vet while you cool the dog, even if they seem to bounce back. The AKC notes that bloodwork is often needed to check for hidden organ damage, including acute kidney injury. Our <a href="/tools/pet-first-aid-guide/">pet first aid guide</a> walks through the cooling steps so you can find them quickly in the moment.</p>

<h2>Putting It Together</h2>

<p>Most panting is a dog being a dog: hot, tired, thrilled, or nervous, and back to normal within minutes. It becomes a concern when there's no trigger, when it doesn't settle with rest and shade, or when it turns into closed-mouth breathing with a working belly. Shaking with no storm outside, a night cough, gums that aren't pink, a dog who won't lie down: those are the details your vet will ask about. And a breath count written down on a calm Tuesday is the best reference you'll have on the night something seems off.</p>

